  "_commit": {
    "id": "e41570622e0e8139e1493e1a001e726f6f4c1889",
    "author": "LTLA <infinite.monkeys.with.keyboards@gmail.com>",
    "committer": "LTLA <infinite.monkeys.with.keyboards@gmail.com>",
    "message": "Overhauled requirements for the fetch.ranges= function.\n\nNow the function is expected to report the start and end positions of the byte\nranges retrieved from the file. This is most relevant to multi-range requests\nwhere the server decides to merge adjacent ranges into a single part. This\nsimplifies the implementation of any fetch.ranges= function, as it doesn't have\nto pick out the individual ranges; that effort would probably be redundant with\nthe range extraction in the consolidation functions anyway.\n\nOn that note, we can refactor downloadMultipartRanges to avoid a great deal of\nwork as it can just return unsorted (and even possibly overlapping) ranges from\nthe response directly. This eliminates a redundant binary search.\n\nThe other binary search (in the consolidation functions) has now been replaced\nwith a linear algorithm that traverses both of the sorted sets of the retrieved\nand requested ranges. This finds the first retrieved range that encloses each\nrequested range, even in the presence of arbitrary nesting within either set of\nranges. This new approach should be a bit faster, but more importantly, be\nrobust to servers that return overlapping ranges.\n\nWe update the single-range request handler in downloadDataseRanges to use\nthe start/end positions in the Content-Range response header, just in case the\nserver decides to slap on more bytes at either the start or the end. We also\nupdate the return value of readDatabaseRanges to report start/end positions.\n",
    "time": 1794143857
  },
